  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This private compatibility module only declares the legacy resource and\n selector address shapes; it has no handwritten executable code. Its derived\n serialization and identity operations do not emit source-line measurements.\n The source-tool argument and delivery regressions in `agent/tests.rs`\n exercise these addresses through the legacy adapter. Supported path-based\n address construction is tested separately in `tests/outline_content.rs`."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on the `crates/trust-but-verify/src/resource.rs` root is on a file root and gives a substantive reason in fenced commentary, so it is a valid coverage-exception request.\n\n- **No executable code.** The file contains only the `ResourceId` and `OutlineId` struct declarations with derives. Nothing handwritten can run, so line coverage is naturally absent or low.\n- **Legacy and private.** `lib.rs` declares `mod resource;` privately and re-exports it only through the `__habeas` module. That matches the \"private compatibility module\" description.\n- **Tests exercise the addresses.** `agent/tests.rs` imports `resource::*`. Several of its tests use the legacy `OutlineId` (with `resource` and `selector`) and `ResourceId` through the source tools. Examples are `source_reads_track_optional_dependencies_and_empty_searches_remain_discovery`, `source_read_pages_retain_text_and_dependencies_without_another_read` and `source_tools_forward_arguments_and_present_discovery_and_read_results`.\n- **Path-based addresses tested separately.** The path-based `outline::OutlineId` (with `path` and `selector`) is tested in `tests/outline_content.rs`, for example in `addresses_reconstruct_without_reading_the_file`. That is a different type from the one in this file, which matches the \"supported path-based\" wording.\n\nThe commentary agrees with the current source. The citation does not also carry an {impl} or {test} reference to `habeas:coverage`, so it is not redundant. I found no gap.",
